course: everyday
lesson: 206
title: Animals outdoors, on the farm and at the zoo
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Animals outside the home are not pets: they protect themselves, their young and their food, so we give them space and stay calm.
explain: You never run from a bear: you stay with adults, speak calmly and back away slowly, because running can make a bear chase you.
explain: Many Australian snakes are venomous; if anyone is bitten by any snake you call the emergency number (000 in Australia) and keep the person calm and still, never sucking or cutting the bite.
explain: A girl in Yorkshire sees a hedgehog in her garden at night; she puts out a shallow dish of water and some meaty cat food, not milk or bread, because milk makes hedgehogs ill.
explain: Sharks rarely attack people, because we are not their normal food; seagulls grab food only because people taught them to by feeding them.
fact: To feed a horse a carrot or apple, you ask the owner, then hold the food on a flat palm so fingers are not mistaken for food.
fact: Some roosters attack people to protect their hens, so you walk away calmly and tell the owner.
fact: A snake in a garden or house in Australia means keep away, keep pets inside and call an adult or a snake catcher.
fact: For any snake bite you call the emergency number and keep the person calm and still.
fact: Sucking or cutting a snake bite does not work and causes harm.
fact: Hedgehogs in the UK and Ireland can have a shallow dish of water or meaty cat food, but never milk or bread, as milk makes them ill.
fact: Raccoons in North America can bite and carry rabies, so you keep away from them and keep bins closed.
fact: If you meet a bear, you stay with adults, do not run, speak calmly and back away slowly, because running can make it chase you.
fact: Feeding seagulls teaches them to snatch food, so you eat in a sheltered spot and do not feed them.
fact: Zoo animals are wild and can be dangerous, so visitors follow the signs, stay behind barriers and never climb fences.
fact: Sharks rarely attack people because humans are not their normal food.
exercise: How do you hold an apple out to a horse after the owner says yes? | On a flat palm | Between your fingertips | In your closed fist | Held in your teeth
exercise: Why do you feed a horse from a flat hand? | So it does not mistake your fingers for food | So the horse can see the apple better before it eats | Because horses only eat from palms | So the apple does not get cold or fall in the mud
exercise: A rooster on your uncle's farm runs at you. What do you do? | Walk away calmly and tell the owner | Chase it back across the yard to show who is boss | Kick it before it reaches you | Pick it up and hold it tight till it calms
exercise: Why might a rooster attack a person? | It may be protecting its hens | Roosters are hungry for people | It wants to be stroked | It thinks you are another rooster's egg
exercise: A boy in Queensland sees a snake on the back step. What is right? | Keep away, keep pets inside and call an adult or a snake catcher | Catch it in a bucket by himself | Poke it with a stick to move it | Take a close photo for his friends
exercise: Someone is bitten by a snake on a bushwalk. What do you do? | Call the emergency number and keep them calm and still | Suck out the venom quickly before it spreads round the body | Cut the bite to let the venom out | Make them run to the car fast so they get to hospital sooner
exercise: Why should nobody suck or cut a snake bite? | It does not work and causes harm | It works only for the small snakes | It is too slow but otherwise helps | Snakes only bite people who move about
exercise: What can you safely put out for a hedgehog in your garden? | A shallow dish of water or meaty cat food | A big bowl of fresh cow's milk every night | Bread soaked in warm milk | Chocolate biscuits and crisps
exercise: Raccoons are in your family's bins at night in Toronto. What do you do? | Keep away and keep the bins closed | Go out and stroke them | Leave food out so they leave the bins | Chase them with a torch
exercise: Why should you stay away from raccoons? | They can bite and carry rabies | They are always friendly pets that like children | They are too slow to escape | They only eat vegetables
exercise: Your family meets a bear on a trail in Colorado. What do you do? | Stay with the adults, speak calmly and back away slowly | Run as fast as you can, because bears are slow on hills | Climb the nearest small tree on your own | Throw your sandwich at it and shout as loud as you can
exercise: Seagulls keep stealing chips at the beach. What helps? | Eat in a sheltered spot and do not feed them | Throw them some chips far away to keep them busy | Wave the chips above your head | Leave your food on the sand while you swim
exercise: At the zoo a sign says "Do not climb on the fence". Why? | Zoo animals are wild and can be dangerous | The paint on the fence is new and still wet | The fence is only there for decoration, not safety | The keepers want you to see less of the animals
exercise: At the aquarium a friend says sharks hunt people. What is true? | Sharks rarely attack people, as we are not their normal food | Sharks eat people whenever they can | Every shark is a danger to swimmers | Sharks only eat people at night
test: Which is true about horses and fingers? | A horse can bite fingers by mistake if food is held in them | Horses never bite anything at all except grass and hay | Horses like being fed by strangers without anyone asking first | Horses only ever bite on purpose
test: Why is giving a hedgehog a saucer of milk a bad idea? | Milk makes hedgehogs ill | Hedgehogs cannot drink at all | Milk makes their spines fall out | Hedgehogs drink only rain water
test: A Canadian family hears a bear near their camp. Which choice could make the bear chase them? | Running away | Backing away slowly | Speaking calmly | Staying together as a group
test: Why do seagulls at some beaches grab food from people's hands? | People fed them and taught them to | Seagulls have no other food anywhere | Seagulls are trained by beach staff | They are angry that people swim in their sea
test: A raccoon walks up to a boy in New Jersey in the daytime. What should he do? | Move away and tell an adult, because raccoons can bite and carry rabies | Offer it a snack from his hand | Pick it up, since it seems tame | Follow it to its nest
test: A dad in Sydney is bitten by a snake. His son should... | Call 000 and keep his dad calm and still | Help him walk quickly to go and find the snake | Cut the bite and suck it out | Put ice on it and do nothing else
test: At the zoo, a child wants to climb over a low wall to get closer to the lions. Why not? | Zoo animals are wild and can kill or hurt people | Lions are only dangerous in Africa, not in zoos at home | The keepers would only be a bit cross | The lions are asleep in the sun, so it is perfectly fine
test: Why do most sharks leave swimmers alone? | People are not their normal food | Sharks are afraid of all colours | Sharks can never see people | Sharks only live in deep tanks
test: A rooster on a farm flaps and charges at a visitor. What does the visitor do? | Walk away calmly and tell the owner | Run off and hide inside the hen house till dark | Grab the rooster by its tail | Shout and stamp at it to scare it off
test: can i give the horse my apple | Ask the owner, then hold it on a flat palm | Hold it in your fingertips so he can see it | Throw it over into the field | Push it right into his mouth, he'll love it
test: theres a snake in the garden! (Brisbane) | Keep away, keep pets in and call an adult or snake catcher | Catch it in a bucket | Poke it with a stick | Throw a shoe at it
test: my uncle says suck the poison out of a snake bite, right? | No, call the emergency number and keep the person calm and still | Yes, suck fast | Yes, and cut the bite first | Yes, then wash with milk
test: hedgehog in the garden, give it milk? | No, milk makes them ill; water or meaty cat food | Yes, warm milk | Yes, milk and bread | Yes, lots of milk
explain: A girl in Colorado climbed into a field to stroke a calf; the mother cow charged, knocked her down and she was badly bruised before a farmer pulled her out.
explain: At a farm park in England a boy fed the goats and then ate his sandwich without washing his hands; he caught a stomach bug from the animals and was very ill for days.
fact: Cows with calves, bulls and horses are big and strong, and every year people are seriously hurt or killed by farm animals.
fact: Farm animals carry germs such as E. coli that can make children very ill, so you wash hands with soap and water after touching them.
test: cows in the field, can we cut through, they look chill | No, cows with calves can charge, go round the field | Yes, cows are gentle | Yes, if you run | Only if you wave to them
test: Leo strokes the lambs at a farm and then wants his crisps. What first? | Wash his hands with soap and water | Wipe them on his trousers | Nothing, lambs are clean | Use the same hands, then wash later
test: zoo fence is low, can i climb it for a selfie with the bear | No, keep behind the fence, wild animals can kill | Yes, if you're quick | Yes, the bear's asleep | Only if your dad holds you
test: did u hear a guy climbed into the lion bit at the zoo for a selfie and got mauled, it's all over tiktok. real lesson? | Stay behind zoo barriers; the animals are wild | Only climb in when the keeper's standing right there | Lions are safe if they've just eaten | Selfies are fine if you're quick about it
test: my mum's mate said her uncle got bit by a snake and they cut it open and sucked the poison out and he lived. true lesson? | No, cutting and sucking harms; call emergency and keep still | Yes, sucking out venom saves lives | Yes, but only if done within a minute | Yes, then walk quickly to a hospital
test: apparently sharks attack like loads of people at our beach every summer, my brother's mate saw one bite a kid's leg off. what's probably exaggerated? | The numbers; shark attacks are rare | That sharks live out there in the sea | That beaches have lifeguards on duty | That some sharks are large
exam: Identify the correct manner in which a carrot is offered to a horse, the owner having consented. | Upon a flat open palm | Held between the fingertips | Thrown upon the ground before it | Held in the mouth of the person
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the appropriate conduct upon encountering a bear? | Remain with adults, speak calmly and retreat slowly without running | Run away as swiftly as possible | Climb the nearest small tree at once | Shout and approach the animal
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the giving of milk to hedgehogs. | Milk makes hedgehogs ill and is not to be given | Milk is the most suitable food for hedgehogs in gardens | Milk is suitable if mixed with bread | Milk may be given only in the winter months
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which hands are to be washed with soap following contact with farm animals. | Farm animals may carry germs such as E. coli | Farm animals dislike the smell of children's unwashed hands | Soap prevents the animals from biting one later | It is required only if the animal was visibly dirty
